<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<UserControl
    x:Class="Rudis.Shell.Regions.PreviewInkLayer"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
      The Canvas region's INK LAYER: an ordinary transparent XAML sibling drawn one
      layer above the Preview region's SwapChainPanel, in the SAME visual tree
      (D-11). It is the whole replacement for src-tauri/src/canvas_input.rs's Win32
      WndProc subclass, which is RETIRED rather than ported — that subclass exists
      only because a Win32 child HWND at HWND_TOP swallowed input the WebView never
      saw, and under a SwapChainPanel that problem does not exist.

      ═══ Background="Transparent" IS LOAD-BEARING ═══

      A null Brush is NOT hit-testable in WinUI: pointer events would fall straight
      through and this region would be silently inert — present in the tree, findable
      by UIA, and incapable of ever receiving a stroke. `Transparent` is a real brush
      that paints nothing and hit-tests everything. Do not "clean this up".

      ═══ WHY THE TRAIL IS DASHED `accent` ═══

      StrokeDashArray + `accent` are not styling choices: they must be pixel-alike
      with what the ENGINE draws for the same stroke once it commits
      (engine::draw_stroke_dashed, with crates/ffi/src/panel/overlay.rs's OVERLAY_INK
      constant — which carries the `accent` token's value on the Rust half; the two
      are kept equal there, and this file states the token name rather than repeating
      the literal, because a hex literal anywhere outside Theme/Tokens.xaml is a build
      FAILURE, comments included). At the hand-off instant both layers briefly draw
      the same geometry in the same place, and that is only invisible if they look
      identical. See PreviewInkLayer.xaml.cs's commit-sequence remarks.

      ═══ HIT-TESTABILITY IS THE TOOL GATE ═══

      InkRoot.IsHitTestVisible is `SelectedTool != Pointer`. With Pointer selected the
      whole layer is inert and every pointer event reaches whatever is beneath it —
      exactly how v6.0's DRAW_MODE flag behaved. LiveTrail is permanently
      IsHitTestVisible="False" so the drawn ink can never eat the pointer that is
      drawing it.

      Every colour is a {StaticResource} token key (CLAUDE.md rule 7 / D-19); a raw
      hex literal here is a build FAILURE (MechanicalGatesTests.
      no_raw_hex_outside_the_token_dictionary).
    -->

    <Grid x:Name="InkRoot"
          Background="Transparent"
          AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Canvas.PreviewInk"
          AutomationProperties.Name="Canvas ink layer">

        <!-- ⚠ A `Path` whose `Data` is REPLACED per update, NOT a `Polyline` whose
             `Points` collection is edited. This is not a style preference — it is
             plan 51-05's own hand-off observation, which caught a live trail that
             rendered NOTHING while every assertion in the C# tier was green:

               · a `Points` value set here in XAML rendered perfectly;
               · clearing that collection from code DID re-render (the shape vanished);
               · adding points to it afterwards drew nothing at all.

             Assigning a fresh `Geometry` to `Path.Data` is a plain DependencyProperty
             set with a new value, which invalidates unambiguously — the same
             mechanism `Canvas.xaml.cs`'s dot grid uses. See RenderTrail. -->
        <Path x:Name="LiveTrail"
              Stretch="None"
              HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
              VerticalAlignment="Stretch"
              Stroke="{StaticResource accent}"
              StrokeThickness="2"
              StrokeDashArray="4,3"
              StrokeLineJoin="Round"
              IsHitTestVisible="False" />
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
